SEC = [
 (193, 'Thinking about the Text – Activity, I', [
  'Activity: Dhanuskodi and Rameswaram are in Ramanathapuram district, Tamil Nadu (Pamban island). Tamil is spoken there. Kalam, his family, friends and teachers spoke Tamil with one another (English was learnt at school).',
  '1) Abdul Kalam’s house was on Mosque Street in Rameswaram – a large pucca house of limestone and brick.',
  '2) Dinamani is the name of a newspaper (a Tamil daily) – because Kalam says he tried to trace the stories of the war in the headlines of Dinamani.',
  '3) His friends were Ramanadha Sastry, Aravindan and Sivaprakasan. | Ramanadha Sastry became the priest of the Rameswaram temple; Aravindan went into business arranging transport for pilgrims; Sivaprakasan became a catering contractor for the Southern Railways.',
  '4) The train halt at Rameswaram was suspended during the war; newspapers were thrown out from the moving train. His cousin Samsuddin needed help to catch the bundles – Kalam did it and earned his first wages.',
  '5) Yes. He collected tamarind seeds (which were in great demand during the war) and sold them to a provision shop on Mosque Street – he got one anna a day.']),
 (194, 'Thinking about the Text – II, III', [
  'II. 1) C) Middle-class, simple, and emotionally secure. | 2) C) By demanding the teacher either apologise or leave the island, which ultimately reformed him. | 3) B) He calmly served Kalam himself and sat beside him to eat.',
  'III. 1 (i) He mentions Muslims and Hindus (Brahmins). Yes, they were identifiable – Kalam wore a cap (Muslim), Ramanadha Sastry wore the sacred thread (Brahmin).',
  '(ii) They shared friendships and experiences naturally. Kalam’s mother and grandmother told bedtime stories from the Ramayana and the life of the Prophet; his three closest friends were Brahmin boys; his family arranged boats with a special platform for carrying idols of the Lord to the Rama Tirtha pond during the Sita Rama Kalyanam festival.',
  '(iii) Aware of differences (and creating them): the new teacher who would not let a Muslim boy sit with a Brahmin boy; Sivasubramania Iyer’s orthodox wife. | Bridging differences: Lakshmana Sastry, Sivasubramania Iyer, Kalam’s parents and the three friends.',
  '(iv) Incident 1: The new teacher moved Kalam to the back bench – difference created; Lakshmana Sastry made the teacher apologise – resolved. | Incident 2: Iyer’s wife refused to serve Kalam in her kitchen; Iyer served him himself and invited him again; she then served Kalam herself – her attitude changed. | People change when they see good examples, when someone stands firmly against injustice, and when they understand others with love.',
  '2 (i) Kalam wanted to leave Rameswaram to study at the district headquarters in Ramanathapuram (higher education). | (ii) His father agreed. He said: “Abul! I know you have to go away to grow. Does the seagull not fly across the sun, alone and without a nest?” and quoted Khalil Gibran to Kalam’s mother. | (iii) To grow, children must leave home like a seagull; parents can give love but not their thoughts – children have their own path. He spoke these words to give Kalam permission and to convince his worried mother.']),
 (194, 'Thinking about Language – I', [
  '1) erupt – a volcano erupts (throws out lava); violence/war erupts (breaks out suddenly); a rash erupts on the skin; a crowd erupts in laughter. | surge – the sea/waves surge; a crowd surges forward; prices surge; a surge of electricity/emotion (sudden increase).',
  '2) trace – (a) to find something after searching (trace a missing person) (b) to copy a drawing (c) a very small amount/mark. | In the text (“I tried to trace the stories of the war in Dinamani”) it means to find/follow – closest to meaning (a).',
  '3) distinguished = famous, respected, special-looking; so undistinguished = ordinary, not special – Kalam says he had “undistinguished looks” (ordinary appearance).']),
 (195, 'Thinking about Language – II', [
  'II.1) (i) broke out – (c) began suddenly in a violent way | (ii) in accordance with – (f) according to a rule | (iii) a helping hand – (d) assistance | (iv) could not stomach – (b) was not able to tolerate | (v) generosity of spirit – (a) kindness, readiness to give | (vi) figures of authority – (e) persons with power to make decisions',
  'II.2) inadequate, unacceptable, irregular, intolerant, undemanding, inactive, untrue, impermanent, unpatriotic, undisputed, inaccessible, incoherent, illogical, illegal, irresponsible, impossible']),
 (196, 'Thinking about Language – IV, V (Passive)', [
  'IV. 1) In yesterday’s competition the prizes were given away by the Principal. | 2) In spite of financial difficulties, the labourers were paid on time. | 3) On Republic Day, vehicles are not allowed beyond this point. | 4) Second-hand books are bought and sold on the pavement every Saturday. | 5) Elections to the Lok Sabha are held every five years. | 6) Our National Anthem was composed by Rabindranath Tagore.',
  'V.1) …Nari Contractor was seriously injured and collapsed. In those days helmets were not worn. Contractor was hit on the head by a bouncer from Charlie Griffith. Contractor’s skull was fractured. The entire team was deeply concerned. The West Indies players were worried. Contractor was rushed to hospital. He was accompanied by Frank Worrell… Blood was donated by the West Indies players. Thanks to the timely help, Contractor was saved. Nowadays helmets are routinely used against bowlers.',
  'V.2) Vegetable oils are made from seeds and fruits… Oil is produced from cotton seeds, groundnuts, soya beans and sunflower seeds. Olive oil is used for cooking, salad dressing etc. Olives are shaken from the trees and gathered up, usually by hand. The olives are ground to a thick paste… Then the mats are layered up on the pressing machine…']),
 (198, 'Speaking, Writing (model)', [
  'Speaking (model points): “Career building is the only goal of education” – I am of the view that education must also build character, values and good citizenship. According to some, a good job brings security; however, quite a few think that a good human being is more important than a good job. If you ask me, both knowledge and goodness are needed.',
  'Writing (model): Life in Rameswaram in the 1940s was simple. Most people were poor or middle-class – fishermen, boat-owners, temple workers and small traders. They worked hard and lived without luxuries. Hindus and Muslims lived side by side and joined in festivals. Society was rigid about caste and religion, yet people like Lakshmana Sastry and Sivasubramania Iyer were hopeful of change. The war brought shortages but also new small jobs. Education was valued, and young people like Kalam dreamt of going out to study and grow.']),
 (200, 'No Men Are Foreign – Thinking about the Poem', [
  '1) (i) Uniforms of soldiers of different countries (also clothes/dress that show nationality, religion or job). | (ii) Beneath the uniforms there is the same human body; we walk on the same earth, are buried in it; we need sun, air and water; our hands work and our eyes wake and sleep alike.',
  '2) Stanza 1: “a single body breathes”, “the land our brothers walk upon is earth like this”, “in which we all shall lie”, “aware of sun and air and water”, “fed by peaceful harvests”, “by war’s long winter starv’d”, “their hands are ours”, “a labour not different from our own”.',
  '3) Stanza 2: “eyes like ours that wake or sleep”, “strength that can be won by love”, “in every land is common life that all can recognise and understand”.',
  '4) This happens during wars, conflicts and riots – leaders, politicians and people with selfish interests tell us to hate others. We should not obey them. The poet says that by hating our brothers we dispossess, betray and condemn ourselves; war defiles the human earth.',
  'II. 1) B) The universal brotherhood of all humans. | 2) C) Despite outward differences, we share the same human biology. | 3) B) War brings harsh suffering and starvation, much like a severe winter. | 4) C) Through love and compassion.']),
]
